+---
+id: dl-001-resolvepointer-semantics-strict-rfc-6901-own-properties-only
+type: decision-log
+title: "resolvePointer semantics: strict RFC 6901, own properties only"
+status: pending
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold verbatim; `memory submit` replaces
+     these placeholder comments with real content and fills the required frontmatter fields. -->
+
+## Context
+
+Other projects will import `resolvePointer` and build on it, so it must follow RFC 6901 exactly.
+
+## Decision
+
+- The pointer is the plain string form (not the URI fragment `#/...` form); the empty string is the whole document.
+- A pointer that is not a string, does not start with `/`, or has a `~` not followed by `0` or `1` throws `SyntaxError`.
+- `~1` is unescaped before `~0`, so `~01` means the key `~1`.
+- Array tokens must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*`; leading zeros, signs and `length` do not resolve. `-` (the nonexistent element after the last) never resolves.
+- Only own properties resolve, so `toString`, `constructor` and `__proto__` are not reachable by inheritance.
+- Any pointer that does not resolve (missing key, out-of-range index, stepping into a scalar or null) throws `RangeError`. A value that is `null` or `undefined`-free falsy still resolves normally.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Callers can tell malformed pointers (`SyntaxError`) from missing values (`RangeError`). Features that
+need to write at `-` (e.g. JSON Patch) will need their own handling, since resolving it throws here.
